# Line-ending policy.
#
# The repo is already LF: of 1416 tracked text blobs, 6 were CRLF and all six
# were the project-template seed under scripts/project_template/_config/. That
# inconsistency is why comparing a live project's _config against the packaged
# scaffold needs `diff --strip-trailing-cr`, and why the Rust migrator carries a
# `newlines` module to normalise before every comparison
# (src/wizard/core/src/migration.rs). This file pins the status quo so the
# mismatch cannot come back.
#
# Deliberately conservative: only file classes positively confirmed safe get a
# rule. Everything unlisted keeps whatever the committer's core.autocrlf does
# today. In particular otls/ (expanded HDA sections, some with base64 payloads),
# *.shelf files are left UNSET on purpose - Houdini owns those files and
# rewrites them itself.

# Source and config: always LF in the repo and in the worktree.
*.py     text eol=lf
*.rs     text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.toml   text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.cfg    text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf

# Shell scripts are executed by sh (Git-Bash on Windows); a CR would end up in
# the interpreted line and break them. The git hooks are the case that matters.
*.sh          text eol=lf
.githooks/*   text eol=lf

# Never touch: binary, or text git must not sniff.
*.hda    binary
*.exe    binary
*.dll    binary
*.so     binary
*.dylib  binary
*.lib    binary
*.pyd    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.ico    binary
*.gz     binary
*.zip    binary
*.pic    binary
*.rat    binary
*.exr    binary
*.usd    binary
*.usdc   binary
